{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/116672-transdimensional-engineering-and-the-world-of-darkness","id":116672,"title":"Transdimensional Engineering and the World of Darkness","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-20T13:50:56-07:00","id":116672,"title":"Transdimensional Engineering and the World of Darkness","content":"So for awhile i've been slowly building a setting for a world of darkness campaign (unsure of new or classic, and unsure of which splat the players would like to play) based around our hometown, a rather small city in upstate New York. Now i had the idea originally to expand the city substantially to accommodate a more dynamic play area, and a higher number of supernatural creatures with which to interact with. That posed a serious problem of actual space, being that our little city is surrounded by a decent sized portion of the Adirondack Park System (great for werewolf games, less so for an expansive cityscape). However a rather interesting solution came to me while reading God Machine Chronicle and Demon the Descent. The city is far larger in the world of darkness, however it manages to occupy the same amount of space, thanks to some rather ingenious spacial engineering. Now in the New World of Darkness this is actually quite an easy feat to achieve story wise (the mechanics aren't a huge deal to me), but in the old world of darkness i'm having a bit of an issue with it. I know an Archmage would have very little trouble accomplishing it, same for any number of other beings of comparable power, but the problem would be keeping it stable. The technocracy would want space to be as it should be, as would the weaver itself. My question is simply how do you think (plot wise) such an area of weird geography would be able to maintain itself without it being &quot;mapped out of existence&quot; by the technocracy, or have reality reassert itself through any of myriad methods?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2778-tlalchitonatiuh","name":"Tlalchitonatiuh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T16:24:32-07:00","id":116721,"title":"","content":"Really the only way you are going to accomplish this in OWoD is by Mages. There are a few options though. This is the kind of thing the Void Engineers would experiment with, and if it is an out of the way place, I could potentially see them taking greater chances for their experiments (Void Engineers are barely technocrats anymore anyway). Another possibility is an expansion into the Umbra, courtesy of Tradition Mages looking for safe harbor. A third (and probably the weirdest, though potentially the coolest) option would be that this town is home to a very powerful Marauder whose particular Reality Bubble expands the entire town as it and everyone else are sucked into his madness fantasy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 20